1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:08,690
Hello, I am Gagan N, USN 24BTRBT013, from
CSE Blockchain Technology, semester five.

2
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:12,902
My mini project for the Corda Development
course is topic number twelve:

3
00:00:12,902 --> 00:00:17,817
Implementation of Secure Peer-to-Peer
Communication between Enterprise Corda Nodes.

4
00:00:17,817 --> 00:00:23,083
In this presentation I will explain the
problem I chose, the Corda concepts I used,

5
00:00:23,083 --> 00:00:27,525
and how I designed the network, the
state, the contract and the flows.

6
00:00:27,525 --> 00:00:32,612
Then I will show how I added end-to-end
encryption and a trusted peer policy, and

7
00:00:32,612 --> 00:00:36,317
the real results I measured on
a running four-node network.

8
00:00:37,076 --> 00:00:39,370
Let me start with the problem.

9
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:44,880
Companies share very sensitive information
with each other, like purchase orders,

10
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:47,351
prices, contract terms and invoices.

11
00:00:47,351 --> 00:00:52,494
Most of this still goes through e-mail,
where messages sit in plain text on many

12
00:00:52,494 --> 00:00:57,830
servers, can be forwarded to anyone, and
give no strong proof of what was received.

13
00:00:57,830 --> 00:01:02,640
Public blockchains are not the answer
either, because they broadcast every

14
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:04,330
transaction to every node.

15
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:09,892
So I needed a way for two enterprises to
talk privately, securely and verifiably,

16
00:01:09,892 --> 00:01:12,226
and only with partners they trust.

17
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:15,149
These were my objectives.

18
00:01:15,099 --> 00:01:21,578
First, I set up a Corda 4.12 network
with a notary and three enterprise nodes.

19
00:01:21,578 --> 00:01:26,755
Second, I designed a secure message
state and a contract with strict rules.

20
00:01:26,755 --> 00:01:35,139
Third, I added application-level
encryption using an X25519 key exchange and

21
00:01:35,139 --> 00:01:36,463
AES-256-GCM.

22
00:01:36,463 --> 00:01:40,797
Fourth, I enforced a trusted-peer
policy from configuration.

23
00:01:40,797 --> 00:01:45,764
And finally, I proved everything with
automated tests and with real evidence from

24
00:01:45,764 --> 00:01:50,302
the running nodes, such as the
certificates, the TLS handshake and latency

25
00:01:50,302 --> 00:01:51,099
measurements.

26
00:01:51,836 --> 00:01:56,744
I chose Corda because it was built for
exactly this kind of business privacy.

27
00:01:56,694 --> 00:02:01,757
In a public blockchain everyone sees
everything and participants are anonymous.

28
00:02:01,757 --> 00:02:07,784
In Corda every node has a known, certified
legal identity, and data is shared only

29
00:02:07,784 --> 00:02:09,548
on a need-to-know basis.

30
00:02:09,548 --> 00:02:14,642
Transactions go directly from one node
to another and are never broadcast.

31
00:02:14,642 --> 00:02:19,965
The notary only confirms uniqueness and
time, and does not need to see the content.

32
00:02:19,965 --> 00:02:25,069
This model gave me a strong base, and
I built extra layers on top of it.

33
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:28,582
These are the Corda concepts I used.

34
00:02:28,532 --> 00:02:32,668
Nodes are the participants, each
with its own vault database.

35
00:02:32,668 --> 00:02:36,553
The network map tells every
node where its peers are.

36
00:02:36,553 --> 00:02:41,075
Flows describe the conversation between
nodes, and sessions are the actual

37
00:02:41,075 --> 00:02:43,397
point-to-point channels inside a flow.

38
00:02:43,397 --> 00:02:49,324
Underneath, Corda uses the Artemis message
broker and the AMQP protocol over TLS.

39
00:02:49,324 --> 00:02:53,972
The notary prevents double spending and
time-stamps transactions, and the vault

40
00:02:53,972 --> 00:02:56,678
stores only the states
a node participates in.

41
00:02:57,428 --> 00:02:59,409
Here is my network.

42
00:02:59,359 --> 00:03:05,090
I have four nodes, created with deployNodes:
a non-validating notary, EnterpriseA in

43
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:09,047
Bengaluru, EnterpriseB in
Mumbai and EnterpriseC in Delhi.

44
00:03:09,047 --> 00:03:14,338
Each node has its own P2P
port, RPC port and RPC user.

45
00:03:14,338 --> 00:03:18,536
EnterpriseA and EnterpriseB
exchange the secure messages.

46
00:03:18,536 --> 00:03:23,718
EnterpriseC is a valid member of the network,
but EnterpriseA does not trust it, so

47
00:03:23,718 --> 00:03:28,151
I can show what happens when an
untrusted peer tries to send a message.

48
00:03:28,892 --> 00:03:31,863
I designed the security as four layers.

49
00:03:31,813 --> 00:03:37,824
The first layer is transport: TLS 1.2 with
mutual authentication between the Artemis

50
00:03:37,824 --> 00:03:38,396
brokers.

51
00:03:38,396 --> 00:03:44,493
The second is identity: both sender and
receiver sign every message with their

52
00:03:44,493 --> 00:03:47,542
Ed25519 keys, and the notary signs too.

53
00:03:47,542 --> 00:03:54,123
The third layer is my own end-to-end
encryption, AES-256-GCM with a fresh key for

54
00:03:54,123 --> 00:03:55,261
every message.

55
00:03:55,261 --> 00:04:00,400
The fourth is privacy: only the two parties
are participants, and a trusted-peer

56
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:02,584
list controls who may talk at all.

57
00:04:03,332 --> 00:04:08,178
This slide shows the certificate
hierarchy that I extracted with keytool.

58
00:04:08,128 --> 00:04:13,232
The Corda root CA signs the Doorman
CA, which signs each node's CA.

59
00:04:13,232 --> 00:04:18,701
The node CA then issues two certificates:
the TLS certificate used on the P2P port,

60
00:04:18,701 --> 00:04:22,128
and the legal identity
certificate used for signing.

61
00:04:22,128 --> 00:04:26,761
When I connected with openssl without
a client certificate, the handshake was

62
00:04:26,761 --> 00:04:28,326
rejected with alert forty.

63
00:04:28,326 --> 00:04:35,580
With a valid network certificate it completed,
using TLS 1.2 and an ECDHE ECDSA AES

64
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:37,065
GCM cipher suite.

65
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:40,676
My SecureMessageState is a linear state.

66
00:04:40,626 --> 00:04:46,117
It stores the sender, the receiver, a
subject, a SHA-256 content hash, the encrypted

67
00:04:46,117 --> 00:04:49,907
payload, two wrapped key
envelopes, the time and a status.

68
00:04:49,907 --> 00:04:53,439
The participants are only
the sender and the receiver.

69
00:04:53,439 --> 00:04:55,585
The contract has two commands.

70
00:04:55,585 --> 00:05:01,659
Send needs no inputs, one SENT output,
different parties, a valid hash, exactly two

71
00:05:01,659 --> 00:05:04,074
participants and both signatures.

72
00:05:04,074 --> 00:05:09,102
Acknowledge changes SENT to ACKNOWLEDGED,
keeps the content unchanged, and must be

73
00:05:09,102 --> 00:05:10,512
signed by the receiver.

74
00:05:11,252 --> 00:05:14,598
This sequence diagram shows my send flow.

75
00:05:14,548 --> 00:05:21,677
First, EnterpriseA checks its trust list
and sends an ephemeral X25519 public key

76
00:05:21,677 --> 00:05:23,173
over the session.

77
00:05:23,173 --> 00:05:28,677
EnterpriseB checks its own trust list,
replies with its public key, and both sides

78
00:05:28,677 --> 00:05:30,287
derive the same AES key.

79
00:05:30,287 --> 00:05:33,621
A encrypts the body and
builds the transaction.

80
00:05:33,621 --> 00:05:37,110
B decrypts it and verifies
the hash before signing.

81
00:05:37,110 --> 00:05:41,964
Then the notary signs, and finality
delivers the transaction only to B.

82
00:05:41,964 --> 00:05:45,464
Later, B reads the message
and acknowledges it.

83
00:05:46,220 --> 00:05:49,535
Let me explain the
encryption in more detail.

84
00:05:49,485 --> 00:05:57,253
For every message, both nodes create fresh
X25519 key pairs, and the shared secret

85
00:05:57,253 --> 00:06:01,422
goes through HKDF to give a 256-bit AES key.

86
00:06:01,422 --> 00:06:06,714
The body is encrypted with AES-GCM,
which also detects any modification.

87
00:06:06,714 --> 00:06:11,839
To decrypt later, each node wraps the key
with a key derived from its own identity

88
00:06:11,839 --> 00:06:14,464
key, so the vault never holds a plain key.

89
00:06:14,464 --> 00:06:19,245
The trust policy is a simple list in
each node's CorDapp configuration file.

90
00:06:19,988 --> 00:06:22,490
This is the technology I used.

91
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:28,846
The CorDapp is written in Java on Corda
4.12 Community Edition, running on JDK 17

92
00:06:28,846 --> 00:06:30,586
and built with Gradle.

93
00:06:30,586 --> 00:06:36,148
For testing I used the MockServices
ledger DSL and MockNetwork with JUnit.

94
00:06:36,148 --> 00:06:40,419
For the security evidence
I used keytool and OpenSSL.

95
00:06:40,419 --> 00:06:45,660
I wrote the helper scripts in Python,
with Pillow and matplotlib for the images,

96
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:50,836
python-docx and python-pptx for the
documents, and ffmpeg with edge-tts for the

97
00:06:50,836 --> 00:06:51,294
videos.

98
00:06:52,052 --> 00:06:53,596
Now the results.

99
00:06:53,546 --> 00:06:58,775
I wrote fifteen contract tests and seven
flow tests, and all twenty-two pass.

100
00:06:58,775 --> 00:07:03,956
The contract tests check cases like a
missing signature, three participants, a bad

101
00:07:03,956 --> 00:07:06,483
hash and changed content on acknowledge.

102
00:07:06,483 --> 00:07:11,112
The flow tests prove the five key
properties: a send and acknowledge works,

103
00:07:11,112 --> 00:07:16,112
EnterpriseC's vault stays empty, a tampered
payload fails the integrity check, an

104
00:07:16,112 --> 00:07:20,556
untrusted peer is rejected, and the
stored payload really is ciphertext.

105
00:07:21,308 --> 00:07:25,029
Here is the real transport
evidence from my running nodes.

106
00:07:24,979 --> 00:07:30,229
The server certificate subject was EnterpriseA
in Bengaluru, and the full chain went

107
00:07:30,229 --> 00:07:31,729
up to the Corda root CA.

108
00:07:31,729 --> 00:07:41,937
The negotiated protocol was TLS 1.2 with
the cipher ECDHE ECDSA AES128 GCM SHA256.

109
00:07:41,937 --> 00:07:46,937
Without a client certificate, the node
refused the handshake and logged an empty

110
00:07:46,937 --> 00:07:48,500
client certificate chain.

111
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:51,885
TLS 1.3 was refused as well.

112
00:07:51,885 --> 00:07:56,979
The node logs also show the AMQP
bridges between A, B and the notary.

113
00:07:57,716 --> 00:08:03,077
In the live run, EnterpriseA sent three
encrypted messages to EnterpriseB, and B

114
00:08:03,077 --> 00:08:04,551
sent one invoice back.

115
00:08:04,501 --> 00:08:09,553
All four were acknowledged, and B decrypted
the purchase order with a matching hash.

116
00:08:09,553 --> 00:08:14,890
The vault counts show the privacy result:
A and B have eight states each, and

117
00:08:14,890 --> 00:08:16,345
EnterpriseC has zero.

118
00:08:16,345 --> 00:08:20,970
EnterpriseC's attempt to message A
was rejected as an untrusted peer.

119
00:08:20,970 --> 00:08:26,984
Twenty pings averaged about 121 milliseconds,
and the key exchange and encryption

120
00:08:26,984 --> 00:08:30,324
added only about one
millisecond per message.

121
00:08:31,076 --> 00:08:36,097
To sum up the advantages: the content
stays protected even if the database or a

122
00:08:36,097 --> 00:08:41,435
backup leaks, uninvolved nodes store nothing,
both parties sign so nobody can deny a

123
00:08:41,435 --> 00:08:45,057
message, tampering is detected,
and the cost is very low.

124
00:08:45,007 --> 00:08:47,268
There are also limitations.

125
00:08:47,268 --> 00:08:52,570
I used development certificates and a single
notary, the keys are in keystore files

126
00:08:52,570 --> 00:08:57,935
and not in a hardware security module, there
is no key rotation yet, and the subject

127
00:08:57,935 --> 00:08:59,341
line is not encrypted.

128
00:09:00,092 --> 00:09:05,321
In conclusion, I built a working CorDapp
in which confidential messages travel over

129
00:09:05,321 --> 00:09:10,549
mutual TLS, are encrypted end-to-end, are
signed by both parties and notarised, and

130
00:09:10,549 --> 00:09:13,636
are readable only by the
sender and the receiver.

131
00:09:13,586 --> 00:09:19,270
All tests pass, and the live network
confirmed privacy, integrity and the trust

132
00:09:19,270 --> 00:09:19,773
policy.

133
00:09:19,773 --> 00:09:25,525
In future I would like to move the keys
into an HSM, add key rotation, and port the

134
00:09:25,525 --> 00:09:29,544
design to Corda 5, where the
MGM manages group membership.

135
00:09:30,284 --> 00:09:32,994
Thank you for listening to my presentation.

136
00:09:32,944 --> 00:09:40,920
The main references I used were the Corda
4.12 documentation, the Corda technical

137
00:09:40,920 --> 00:09:48,601
white paper, and the standards for X25519,
HKDF, AES-GCM, SHA-256 and TLS 1.2.

138
00:09:48,601 --> 00:09:54,059
The full code, the test results and all the
evidence files are in my project folder.

139
00:09:54,059 --> 00:09:58,799
I am happy to take any questions about
the design or the implementation.

